index-ui §3.2: opening a result scrolls to the matching chunk's heading. It silently lands at the top when:
- the heading has inline markup (the chunker keeps the raw `` `foo` ``, the renderer shows `foo`);
- the heading has HTML entities (kept raw by the chunker, decoded by the renderer);
- the document is code or plain text (those renderers put no heading ids at all).

**Done when:** heading matching normalises both sides the same way, code and plain-text views anchor on the chunk's span, and a view test covers each case.

There is no way to forget anything: no `kb` command for index-api §2's `DELETE /documents/{id}` or `DELETE /sources/{id}`, and the document log has no forget record. As a result `kb collections delete` always refuses with `collection_in_use` for any collection that holds documents — which is every real one.

**Done when:**
- the document log gains a forget record (append-only, like the source log's), and the fold drops forgotten documents;
- `kb forget D-n` / `kb forget S-n` exist (forgetting a source forgets its documents), with `--json`;
- links to a forgotten document keep reading as `resolved: false` (§6 already promises this);
- `kb compact` then drops the orphaned blobs;
- `collections delete` works once a collection is emptied, and Knowledge's collection "Forget" can offer to forget its documents first;
- kb-js gets the bindings; not exposed through MCP (§9: forgetting is the reader's decision).

index-api §2's source routes do not exist: no `GET /sources`, `GET /sources/{id}`, `POST /sources` or `POST /sources/{id}/refresh`. `json_source` in `cmd_common.c` is dead code. Knowledge builds its `kb:/S-n` page from `ls --source` as a workaround.

Scope, per the decision that kb holds reference material and never the workspace's own tree:
- `kb sources [--collection] [--kind]` and `kb sources show S-n` (with its documents and fetch history);
- file ingest by locator (`kind: file`); **no directory walk** — dropped, see the spec ticket;
- per-source refresh for `file` sources (re-read, compare by hash, re-index only what changed); `url` stays "re-file through kb add" while §12.2 keeps HTTP out of the binary.

**Done when:** the routes exist with `--json`, kb-js binds them, Knowledge's source page uses them, and e2e covers refresh-by-hash.

`embed.c`, `gguf.c`, `quant.c` and `wpm.c` are written but not in the Makefile or either CMakeLists, and no command calls them. The forward pass was checked against the llama.cpp oracle (cosine 0.985–0.995) but has no tests of its own. The model is found in `~/.kb/models` (exactly one `.gguf`, read-only; `model_missing` prints the curl command).

Note: `embed.c`/`embed.h` still hold the user's uncommitted in-progress work; start by committing that with them.

**Done when:**
- the four files build with the rest, with unit tests for the GGUF reader, k-quant dequantisation and tokenizer;
- `index/model.json` records the §8 fields (model, dim, pooling, maxTokens, prefixes, normalize, chunkTokens, chunkOverlap, quantization) plus the model file's hash;
- `kb status` reports the model instead of `"model": null`;
- a store whose recorded model differs from the loaded one refuses search with `model_mismatch` until `kb reindex`.

Blocked by T-10. index-api §8: vectors stored int8 in `index/vectors.bin` and scanned flat (exact SIMD scan, no approximate index), with the asymmetric query/document prefixes applied.

**Done when:**
- ingest, `rebuild` and `reindex` embed chunks into `vectors.bin`;
- `--mode semantic` works, and `hybrid` fuses keyword and vector lists by rank (the RRF seam in `rank.c` already exists) and becomes the default;
- hits carry `scores.vector` and `matched: ["keyword","semantic"]` when both paths found them;
- e2e runs against the real model when `~/.kb/models` has it and skips cleanly otherwise.

Agreed with the user on 2026-09-26: kb holds what was *read* (docs, papers, reference code at a pinned version), never the code being *written* — that is always changing, and grep/LSP already answer it exactly and currently.

**Done when `specs/index-api.md` says so:**
- §1's opening: "documentation, *reference* source and papers";
- §2: drop `kind: dir` (a directory walk pointed at `.` would index the project); a file at a time is enough;
- §12.2 (fetching) and the refresh wording reflect that the CLI has no HTTP client.

Today "procode: Set Up MCP for Claude Code" writes the project's `.mcp.json`: per project, machine-specific paths (so it cannot be committed), and it goes stale on every update.

Proposed:
- register `coboard` and `kb` at Claude Code's **user** scope (`claude mcp add-json <name> '<entry>' --scope user`), so every project has them and nothing is written into repositories;
- offer it once on first start when the `claude` CLI is found; after that, rewrite our own two user-scope entries after every update, silently;
- without the CLI, copy the two ready commands to the clipboard;
- keep the project-scope command for anyone who wants it.

**Verify first:** the exact `claude mcp add-json` syntax, and that user-scope stdio servers start in the project directory (they must, to find each project's `.coboard/` and `.kb/`).

